% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup
% Note: eq:ii and eq:gradient are the canonical definitions.
% Full formulation details in Appendix (app:formulation-details).

\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.}
For a text segment, let $\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \text{PPL}_c / \text{PPL}_d$, and:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null ($\mathrm{II} = 0$ under no interaction) as the primary formulation. Although the two sparsity axes are causally coupled---head pruning alters the importance scores that KV eviction relies on (\S\ref{sec:analysis})---the additive null provides the most \emph{conservative} baseline: it assumes compound loss equals the sum of individual losses ($r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$), the weakest independence assumption. If even this permissive null is rejected ($\mathrm{II} > 0$), the interaction is super-additive regardless of null specification. Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$), which yields larger amplification estimates ($G_{\times} = 58$--$241{\times}$), confirming that our conclusions are null-choice invariant. Because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed, we report medians throughout.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture phase variation, the per-problem \emph{amplification ratio} is:
\begin{equation}
    G^{(i)} = \frac{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{high}}},
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G \gg 1$ indicates phase-concentrated severity.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
\label{par:phase-segmentation}
Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\section{Compound Sparsity Formulation}
\label{app:formulation-details}

\paragraph{Attention head pruning.}
Heads are ranked by Frobenius norm importance $w_j^{(\ell)} = \|\mathbf{A}_j^{(\ell)}\|_F$ \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}; a binary mask $m_j^{(\ell)} \in \{0,1\}$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\cdot H$ heads:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathbf{o}}_j^{(\ell)} = m_j^{(\ell)} \cdot \mathbf{o}_j^{(\ell)}.
    \label{eq:head-mask}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{KV cache eviction.}
A position mask $\mathbf{m}_k \in \{0,1\}^T$ retains the top-$(1{-}s)\cdot T$ positions (BOS always preserved):
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{a}_{ij} = a_{ij} \cdot m_{k,j}, \quad \forall\, i, j.
    \label{eq:kv-mask}
\end{equation}
Under importance-based eviction, each position $j$ is scored by its cumulative attention mass with position normalization:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{imp}(j) = \frac{\sum_{\ell, h, i} a_{ij}^{(\ell,h)}}{T - j},
    \label{eq:kv-importance}
\end{equation}
where the denominator compensates for causal bias---earlier positions are attended to by more queries \cite{thinkv-iclr2026, defensivekv-iclr2026}. Under random eviction, positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Applying both mechanisms simultaneously yields compound sparsity. The two axes interact: head pruning redistributes attention mass, altering the importance ranking for KV eviction. Prior work \cite{spark-aaai2026} reports super-additive joint effects but treats interaction magnitude as a fixed property of $s$.

\paragraph{Attention sparsity metric.}
For each query position $i$ and head $h$, we compute the fraction of key positions receiving negligible attention:
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_i^{(h)} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{j=1}^{T} \mathbf{1}\!\left[a_{ij}^{(h)} < 0.01 \cdot \max_k a_{ik}^{(h)}\right].
    \label{eq:attn-sparsity}
\end{equation}
The per-position sparsity $\sigma_i = \frac{1}{H}\sum_h \sigma_i^{(h)}$ averages across all heads. Tokens are partitioned into high-, mid-, and low-sparsity phases by the tercile boundaries of $\{\sigma_i\}$; segments shorter than 10 tokens are merged into adjacent phases.

\section{Multiplicative Null Verification}
\label{app:multiplicative-null}

We verified that replacing the additive null $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}) with the multiplicative null $r_b = r_a \cdot r_k$ does not change qualitative conclusions. Under the multiplicative formulation, the interaction index becomes $\mathrm{II}_{\times} = r_b - r_a \cdot r_k$. Across all sparsity levels and both eviction strategies, the sign and relative ordering of $\mathrm{II}_{\times}$ match those of the additive II: importance-based eviction exhibits a monotonically increasing severity gradient ($G_{\times} = 58$--$241{\times}$), while random eviction remains flat ($G_{\times} \approx 1$). The crossover at $s{=}0.25$ is preserved. We adopt the additive formulation in the main text as the more conservative baseline: it assumes zero interaction (compound loss equals the sum of individual losses), so rejection ($\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. The qualitative conclusions---phase-modulated severity, strategy asymmetry, and crossover---are preserved under the multiplicative null.

