\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Amplification ratio ($G$) as a function of sparsity level $s$ on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}496$). Importance-based eviction (solid) exhibits an increasing gradient from $7.5{\times}$ to $79.8{\times}$; random eviction (dashed) remains near $1{\times}$ ($0.5{\times}$--$1.1{\times}$). Shaded regions show bootstrap 95\% CIs. Per-phase breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:main-results}.}
\label{fig:hero}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Phase-Strategy Crossover}
\label{sec:crossover}

For DeepSeek, a strategy ranking reversal emerges at all four sparsity levels ($n{=}496$): at $s{=}0.25$, importance-based eviction yields \emph{lower} II than random in the high-sparsity phase ($0.021$ vs.\ $1.113$) but \emph{higher} II in the low-sparsity phase ($3.971$ vs.\ $1.514$---a $2.6{\times}$ reversal; both $p < 10^{-4}$), with reversal magnitude ranging from $2.2{\times}$ to $4.0{\times}$ across $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$. Qwen does not exhibit this crossover: importance-based eviction produces lower II than random in both phases (e.g., at $s{=}0.25$: high $0.004$ vs.\ $1.108$; low $1.053$ vs.\ $1.769$), indicating that the ranking reversal is model-dependent. Full crossover analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{app:crossover-details}.

\subsection{Replication Across Training Paradigms}
\label{sec:cross-model}

DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (distillation-trained) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (instruction-tuned) share the Qwen2 backbone but differ in training objective, enabling controlled comparison of training paradigm effects.
Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct ($n_{\text{eff}}{=}475$) reproduces the monotonic severity gradient: $\Delta$ increases from $0.07$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $3.16$ at $s{=}0.30$ (all Bonferroni-corrected $p < 10^{-14}$), paralleling DeepSeek at approximately one-quarter to one-sixth the magnitude. Both models yield strictly increasing median $\Delta$ trajectories (Jonckheere-Terpstra $z \geq 6.2$, $p < 10^{-9}$; see \S\ref{par:stats}). Random eviction on Qwen mirrors DeepSeek: non-significant at $s \leq 0.20$, becoming significant at $s \geq 0.25$ ($\Delta{=}0.39$--$0.91$; $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$) but $2.7$--$20{\times}$ weaker than importance-based. A caveat applies to mid-phase comparisons: on Qwen, 45.2\% of problems yield null mid-phase segments due to low temporal autocorrelation in attention sparsity (lag-1 $r{=}0.718$ vs.\ DeepSeek's $0.894$; $d{=}-1.68$), preventing formation of contiguous mid-phase runs above the $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}{=}10$ threshold. This does not affect the primary low--high contrast ($\Delta$). Per-phase effective sample sizes are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:qwen-null-rate}. Full cross-training-paradigm tables appear in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-details}.

\subsection{Exploratory Cross-Architecture Analysis}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

Cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, $n{=}49$)\footnote{The $n{=}49$ sample is the profiling subset; full-scale ($n{=}500$) replication on LlamaForCausalLM was deprioritized in favor of the primary Qwen2 analysis.} confirms the phase-gated severity pattern for importance-based eviction (post-hoc power $> 0.99$ for within-importance gradient) but lacks statistical power ($0.26$--$0.63$) for strategy comparisons; the interaction magnitude is ${\sim}40{\times}$ smaller than DeepSeek, suggesting that quantitative characteristics may be Qwen2-family-specific and that broader architectural diversity (e.g., Mistral, Gemma) is needed for generalization claims. Full results, including per-sparsity breakdowns and a power analysis motivating the sample size needed for definitive cross-architecture claims, appear in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-arch}.

\subsection{Statistical Validation}
\label{sec:stat-validation}

Importance-based eviction produces significantly larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction at all four sparsity levels: the median paired difference (importance $-$ random) increases from $0.42$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.26$ at $s{=}0.30$ (all $p_{\text{Bonf}} \ll 10^{-10}$, Wilcoxon signed-rank). Full permutation test results from the $n{=}50$ profiling subset appear in Appendix~\ref{app:permutation-details}.

Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports the paired median difference $\Delta$ under importance-based eviction for both models at $n{=}496$/$475$. All eight conditions yield CIs excluding zero. The within-importance dose-response $d_z$ (s015 vs.\ s030) is $0.94$ for DeepSeek and $0.92$ for Qwen, both large effects.\footnote{At $n{=}50$ profiling, per-condition $d_z$ ranged from $0.44$--$0.80$ (DS) and $0.28$--$1.02$ (QW); $d_z$ instability at high sparsity due to outlier-driven variance resolved at $n{=}500$.}

